     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Dagger 2.48 release notes: "includes the alpha release of the Dagger and Hilt KSP processors" (github.com/google/dagger
     releases); Hilt component docs as in components.blend. Checked 2026-10-25. Koin's current verification docs distinguish the Compiler Plugin from the Classic DSL without that plugin. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">Two teams ship the same feature. Team A's app crashes for 2% of users the first time they open Settings: a dependency was never registered, and nothing noticed until runtime. Team B never sees that crash, but its incremental build takes forty seconds longer, and the team argues about whether that is worth it.</p>

Both teams are doing dependency injection; they chose different machines for it. Manual DI, Hilt and Koin all pass collaborators in from outside. They differ in **when the graph is checked** (build time or first use), **what it costs to build**, and **where it can run** (Android only, or shared Kotlin). This chapter compares them on those axes so the choice is a trade-off you name, not a habit.

<!-- @after Why DI Matters (Briefly) :: visual -->
### Who asks, and who is told

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Dependency injection</span><div class="node"><strong><code>CheckoutViewModel(repo: UserRepository)</code></strong><small>needs are visible in the constructor</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Test</strong><small>pass a fake directly; no container involved</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Service locator</span><div class="node"><strong><code>val repo = container.get&lt;UserRepository&gt;()</code></strong><small>needs are hidden inside the class body</small></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Test</strong><small>configure the global container before the class can even be built</small></div></div></div><figcaption>The difference is not the library: Koin used with <code>get()</code> inside classes is a locator; Koin with constructor injection is DI.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @before Option 2: Hilt (Dagger Wrapped) :: review -->
Three passages are under technical review: the ViewModel row of the Hilt scope table, the KSP status in The Build-Time Cost, and the Common Pitfalls item about eagerly wired `@Singleton` graphs. A correction is pending the owner's approval.

<!-- @after Manual DI vs Hilt vs Koin: Side-by-Side :: animation -->
### When does a missing dependency get caught?

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">The same mistake under three approaches</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:3"><div class="node" data-el="manual" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Manual DI</strong><small data-text="manualV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="hilt" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Hilt</strong><small data-text="hiltV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="koin" data-art="graph" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Koin DSL without compiler checks</strong><small data-text="koinV">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"The mistake","caption":"A new SettingsViewModel needs a PrivacyRepository. The developer forgets to wire it.","add":["manual","hilt","koin"],"state":{},"text":{"manualV":"AppContainer has no privacyRepository","hiltV":"no @Inject constructor, @Provides or @Binds","koinV":"no single { PrivacyRepository(…) }"}},
{"label":"Compile","caption":"Manual DI fails to compile, because the factory must pass a real object to the constructor. Hilt fails too: Dagger checks the whole graph and reports a missing binding. In this example the Koin DSL is used without compiler-plugin validation, so the missing definition survives compilation.","state":{"manual":"done","hilt":"done","koin":"warn"},"text":{"manualV":"✗ build fails: no value to pass","hiltV":"✗ build fails: [Dagger/MissingBinding]","koinV":"✓ compiles"}},
{"label":"Ship","caption":"Without a verification step, this Koin build reaches users; opening Settings then requests the missing definition.","state":{"koin":"error"},"text":{"koinV":"runtime missing-definition exception when Settings opens"}},
{"label":"Mitigate","caption":"Add verification for a Classic DSL graph, or enable the Koin Compiler Plugin's supported graph checks. The annotations processor also has a KOIN_CONFIG_CHECK option. Validation coverage depends on the configuration.","state":{"koin":"warn","manual":"","hilt":""},"text":{"manualV":"cost: wiring by hand","hiltV":"cost: annotation processing time","koinV":"cost: a verification step you must remember"}}
]</script><figcaption>Every approach catches the mistake somewhere. The question is whether that place is your build or your user's device.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after When Each Wins :: check -->
**Pick the approach.** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>A payments SDK distributed to other companies' apps.</summary>

Manual DI. Your consumers must not inherit your DI framework, its version or its annotation processing.
</details>

<details><summary>An Android-only app with 60 feature modules and many contributors.</summary>

Hilt. Build-time graph validation and lifecycle-matched components pay for their build cost at that size.
</details>

<details><summary>A Kotlin Multiplatform app sharing repositories with iOS.</summary>

Koin, or manual DI, in the shared code. Hilt is Android-only and cannot run in shared iOS code.
</details>

